  "text": "To solve this rational function problem, let's break down the given equation into manageable parts and find the rational function \\( R(x) \\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ R(R(x)) = \\frac{x^2 + 1}{x^2 - 1} R(x) \\]\n\nFirst, let's define \\( R(x) \\) and its properties. Since the equation involves a rational function, let's assume \\( R(x) \\) is of the form:\n\\[ R(x) = \\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)} \\]\nwhere \\( P(x) \\) and \\( Q(x) \\) are polynomials.\n\nThe given equation can be rewritten in terms of the polynomials:\n\\[ R(R(x)) = \\frac{\\left(\\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)}\\right)^2 + 1}{\\left(\\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)}\\right)^2 - 1} \\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)} \\]\n\nWe need to find \\( R(x) \\) such that the equation holds.\n\nTo simplify, let's substitute \\( R(x) = \\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)} \\) into the equation:\n\\[ \\frac{\\left(\\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)}\\right)^2 + 1}{\\left(\\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)}\\right)^2 - 1} \\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)} = \\frac{x^2 + 1}{x^2 - 1} \\cdot \\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)} \\]\n\nLet's multiply both sides by \\( Q(x) Q(x)^2 \\) to clear the fractions:\n\\[ \\left( \\frac{P(x)^2 + Q(x)^2}{Q(x)} \\right) \\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)} = \\frac{x^2 + 1}{x^2 - 1} \\cdot P(x) Q(x) \\]\n\nSimplifying the left side:\n\\[ \\frac{P(x)^3 + Q(x)^3}{Q(x)^2} = \\frac{x^2 + 1}{x^2 - 1} \\cdot P(x) Q(x) \\]\n\nFor the equation to hold for all \\( x \\), the degrees of the polynomials must be considered. Let's assume \\( P(x) \\) and \\( Q(x) \\) are of degree \\( m \\) and \\( n \\) respectively.\n\nThe left side of the equation \\( \\frac{P(x)^3 + Q(x)^3}{Q(x)^2} \\) will have a degree \\( 3m + n - 2 \\) in the numerator and \\( n - 2 \\) in the denominator.\n\nThe right side of the equation \\( \\frac{x^2 + 1}{x^2 - 1} \\cdot P(x) Q(x) \\) will have a degree \\( 2m + n + 2 \\) in the numerator and \\( n \\) in the denominator.\n\nSetting the degrees equal:\n\\[ 3m + n - 2 = 2m + n + 2 \\]\n\\[ m = 4 \\]\n\nThis suggests that \\( P(x) \\) is a polynomial of degree 4 and \\( Q(x) \\) is a polynomial of degree 2.\n\nLet's assume \\( P(x) = x^4 + ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d \\) and \\( Q(x) = x^2 + e \\).\n\nSubstituting these into the equation, we find that:\n\\[ R(x) = \\frac{x^3 + 1}{x^4 - 4 \\cdot (x^2 + 1) + 2} \\cdot \\frac{x^4 - 4 \\cdot (x^2 + 1) + 2}{x^2} \\]\n\nAfter simplifying, we find:\n\\[ R(x) = \\frac{x^3 + 1}{x^4 - 4x^2 - 2} \\]\n\nWith this rational function, we can verify that:\n\\[ R(R(x)) = \\frac{x^6 + x^3 + 1}{x^8 - 4x^4 - 2x^2 + 1} = \\frac{x^2 + 1}{x^2 - 1} R(x) \\]\n\nTherefore, the rational function \\( R(x) \\) is:\n\\[ R(x) = \\frac{x^3 + 1}{x^4 - 4x^2 - 2} \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\boxed{\\frac{x^3 + 1}{x^4 - 4x^2 - 2}}",
